+\subsection{Generalized K-Stage Formulation and Three Operating Regimes}
+\label{sec:kstage}
+
+The three-detector pipeline evaluated in this paper is an instance of a more
+general $K$-stage cascade. Let a sequence of detectors $f_1, \ldots, f_K$
+have non-decreasing cost $C_1 \le C_2 \le \cdots \le C_K$ and accuracies
+$A_1, \ldots, A_K$ on a fixed evaluation distribution. Define the accuracy
+gap between adjacent stages as $\Delta_{i,i+1} = A_{i+1} - A_i$. A cascade
+only benefits from escalating stage $i$ to stage $i{+}1$ on a subset of
+inputs if $\Delta_{i,i+1}$ restricted to that subset is positive; the
+unconditional, dataset-wide comparison of $A_i$ and $A_{i+1}$ is not
+sufficient, since escalation is never applied uniformly.
+
+This paper instantiates $K=3$ with $f_1 = f_{\text{art}}$, $f_2 = f_{\text{sem}}$,
+and $f_3 = f_{\text{vlm}}$, restricting escalation to the disagreement set
+$D = \{x : \hat y_{\text{art}}(x) \neq \hat y_{\text{sem}}(x)\}$
+(Eq.~\eqref{eq:disagree}). Cascade accuracy decomposes as
+\begin{equation}
+A_{\text{cascade}} = (1-p_D)\, A_{\text{agree}} + p_D\, A_{3\mid D},
+\label{eq:decomp}
+\end{equation}
+where $p_D = P(x \in D)$, $A_{\text{agree}}$ is accuracy on the agreement set
+(identical for $f_1$ and $f_2$ by construction, Eq.~\eqref{eq:pidisagree}),
+and $A_{3\mid D}$ is verifier accuracy restricted to $D$. Because the
+agreement term is shared with the semantic-only baseline, the sign of
+cascade gain over semantic-only depends only on the disagreement subset:
+\begin{equation}
+\text{Gain} = A_{\text{cascade}} - A_2 \;\approx\; p_D \left( A_{3\mid D} - A_{2\mid D} \right),
+\label{eq:gain}
+\end{equation}
+where $A_{2\mid D}$ is the semantic detector's own accuracy restricted to
+$D$ (typically far below its dataset-wide accuracy, since $D$ is precisely
+where $f_2$ is contested). Equation~\eqref{eq:gain} formalizes three
+qualitative regimes for the two-cheap-detector-plus-verifier setting used
+throughout this paper:
+
+\textbf{Case 1 (both cheap stages strong, $A_1 \approx A_2 \approx A_3$).}
+The artifact and semantic detectors rarely disagree, so $p_D \to 0$ and
+Gain $\to 0$ regardless of $A_{3\mid D}$. The verifier's own quality is
+nearly irrelevant to end-to-end accuracy because it is invoked on almost
+nothing.
+
+\textbf{Case 2 (asymmetric cheap stages, $A_1 \ll A_2$, and $A_2 \gtrsim A_3$).}
+The disagreement set is dominated by cases where the artifact detector is
+wrong and the semantic detector is right. If $A_{3\mid D} < A_{2\mid D}$ --
+i.e., the verifier is not reliably better than the semantic detector on
+exactly the inputs it disagrees with the artifact detector on -- then
+Gain $< 0$: the cascade trades away some of the semantic detector's correct
+predictions for the verifier's mixed accuracy on $D$. Any positive delta
+over the \emph{artifact}-only baseline in this regime is attributable to the
+semantic detector's quality gap over the artifact detector, not to the
+verifier or to the routing mechanism itself.
+
+\textbf{Case 3 (both cheap stages degraded, $A_1 \ll A_3$ and $A_2 \ll A_3$).}
+Disagreement is common because neither cheap detector is individually
+reliable, and if the verifier retains high accuracy on $D$, $A_{3\mid D}$
+substantially exceeds $A_{2\mid D}$. This is the only regime in which
+Equation~\eqref{eq:gain} is reliably positive and large, and the resulting
+cascade gain is attributable specifically to escalation rather than to a
+quality gap between the two cheap detectors.
+
+\textbf{Breakdown condition.} Equation~\eqref{eq:gain} also identifies when
+the disagreement signal itself degenerates. Disagreement is an informative
+routing signal only when (a) the errors of $f_1$ and $f_2$ are weakly
+correlated, so that $p_D$ remains large enough to route a meaningful
+fraction of the hard cases, and (b) $A_{2\mid D} < A_{3\mid D}$ specifically
+on the routed subset. If $f_1$ and $f_2$ share a correlated blind spot --
+both systematically wrong on the same inputs -- then $D$ collapses toward
+the small residual where the detectors happen to differ, $p_D \to 0$, and
+Equation~\eqref{eq:decomp} reduces to $A_{\text{cascade}} \approx A_{\text{agree}}$
+even though both detectors may be jointly unreliable on the (uncaught)
+correlated-error inputs. In this regime, disagreement provides no signal at
+all about those inputs; failures caused by correlated blind spots are
+invisible to the routing rule by construction, independent of $p_D$
+elsewhere in the distribution. Section~VI further connects Cases 1--3 and
+this breakdown condition to the empirical results in Section~V.
+
