+\subsection{Three Regimes in Practice}
+\label{sec:three_regimes}
+
+The K-stage formulation (Section~III.D) predicts three qualitative regimes, and each is
+directly realized in the empirical results above. \textbf{Case 1} (both cheap stages
+strong) is BigGAN: FFT alone reaches 99.8\% and disagreement collapses below 1\%, so the
+cascade adds latency without changing accuracy (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). \textbf{Case 2}
+(asymmetric cheap stages) is the in-domain GenBuster and SD~1.4 result (Section~V.B) and,
+more sharply, the cross-family deployment experiment
+(Table~\ref{tab:cross_family_deployment}, Section~V.D.5): a badly cross-family-transferred
+SigLIP2 probe creates a large apparent cascade gain over itself, but the cascade beats the
+FFT detector alone in only one of six pairings, showing the gain is a detector-quality-gap
+artifact rather than genuine routing value. \textbf{Case 3} (both cheap stages degraded) is
+the SD~1.4-to-SDXL/FLUX shift with GPT-5.5 as verifier (Table~\ref{tab:e8_results}): both
+the stale semantic probe and, to a lesser extent, FFT lose accuracy under this shift, and
+escalation recovers up to 4.9 points with consistent positive gain across five of six
+configurations (Figure~\ref{fig:cascade_gain}). Crucially, this Case~3 status is
+verifier-dependent, not a property of the routing mechanism alone: substituting Qwen2.5-VL-7B
+for GPT-5.5 as the verifier on the identical SDXL/FLUX disagreement subsets
+(Table~\ref{tab:e8_results}) turns every one of the six configurations negative
+($-$3.0 to $-$14.0 points), reverting the same degraded-cheap-detector setting to a
+Case~2-like outcome. Both cheap detectors being weak is necessary but not sufficient for
+Case 3; the verifier must also be reliably stronger than the stronger cheap detector on
+the routed subset, exactly as Equation~\eqref{eq:gain} requires.
+
+The probe-degradation sweep (Table~\ref{tab:limited_data}, holding FFT fixed while the
+semantic probe's training fraction shrinks from 100\% to 1\%) directly demonstrates the
+Case~2-to-Case~3 transition as a continuous function of semantic-probe quality, rather
+than relying on cross-dataset anecdote. On SD~1.4 with a DINOv2 probe, cascade gain over
+semantic-only is $-$4.8 points at 100\% training data (Case 2) and crosses to $+$10.0
+points at 1\% training data (Case 3), passing through near-zero around 5--10\% data; the
+SigLIP2 backbone shows the same sign change between 1\% and 5\% data. On GenBuster, by
+contrast, gain remains negative at every fraction tested, since even a 1\%-trained
+semantic probe (16 labeled examples) already exceeds the FFT detector's accuracy on that
+benchmark -- illustrating that the transition point depends on the FFT detector's own
+strength on a given generator family, not on data quantity alone.
+
+The \textbf{breakdown condition} (Section~III.D) -- disagreement degenerating when the two
+cheap detectors share a correlated blind spot -- is realized directly in
+Table~\ref{tab:escalation}: on GenBuster, the single case where FFT is wrong but the
+semantic probe agrees with it (both detectors sharing the same error) is invisible to the
+routing rule by construction, representing the fundamental limit of disagreement-based
+routing at under 0.3\% of inputs.
