+\subsection{A Practical Fail-Safe: the Verifier Validation Gate}
+\label{sec:validation_gate}
+
+A deployed system does not know in advance whether an incoming distribution is
+in-domain, a benign generator update, or a hostile shift, so the guideline above --
+prefer FFT-only under cross-family shift, prefer the GPT-5.5 cascade under SD~1.4-to-SDXL/FLUX
+shift -- cannot be applied directly without first knowing which regime holds. A
+natural first attempt is to use the disagreement rate itself as this signal, escalating
+only when it exceeds the $\sim$26--28\% in-domain baseline (Section~V.C). This does not
+work: the cross-family disagreement rate (Case~2, where escalation to Qwen hurts in five
+of six pairings) ranges 38.5--50.4\%, and the SDXL/FLUX disagreement rate (Case~3, where
+escalation to GPT-5.5 helps in five of six configurations) ranges 35.2--41.4\%
+(Table~\ref{tab:e8_efficiency}) -- these ranges overlap substantially, so disagreement
+rate alone cannot distinguish a regime where escalation helps from one where it hurts.
+
+We instead propose a \textbf{verifier validation gate}: before deploying escalation to a
+verifier $V$ on the disagreement set of a new detector pair or a new target
+distribution, evaluate $V$'s accuracy on a small labeled calibration sample drawn from
+that disagreement set. Deploy the cascade only if $A_{V,\text{cal}} >
+\max(A_1, A_2)$ measured on the same calibration sample; otherwise, fall back to
+whichever cheap detector is individually stronger. This rule requires no knowledge of
+whether the deployment is in-domain or shifted -- it only requires a calibration sample
+from wherever the system is currently running, which a practitioner can always collect
+regardless of whether the distribution's identity is known.
+
+We validate this rule retrospectively against every configuration evaluated in this
+paper where ground truth is available: the six cross-family pairings with Qwen
+(Table~\ref{tab:cross_family_deployment}), and the six SDXL/FLUX configurations under
+each of GPT-5.5 (Table~\ref{tab:e8_results}) and Qwen (Table~\ref{tab:e8_results}), 18
+configurations total. Using the full disagreement-subset accuracy as an oracle
+calibration sample, the gate's escalate/fall-back decision matches the actual outcome
+(whether the cascade beat the stronger cheap detector) in 17 of 18 configurations
+(94.4\%); the single miss, FLUX with a SigLIP2 probe and GPT-5.5 as verifier, is a
+near-exact tie (90.88\% vs.\ 90.93\%, a 0.04-point gap). The gate correctly identifies
+that Qwen should not be trusted as a verifier in any of the twelve configurations
+tested (six cross-family, six SDXL/FLUX), and correctly identifies that GPT-5.5 should
+be trusted in five of six SDXL/FLUX configurations, without ever being told which
+generator family, which verifier, or which shift type it was evaluating. In deployment,
+the calibration sample would be smaller than the full disagreement subset and introduce
+some estimation noise; we report the oracle version here as an upper bound on the
+rule's achievable reliability, and leave calibration-sample-size sensitivity to future
+work.
